

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Explicit watch list. That also means the tasks core needs a [38;5;153msubscriptions[39m table, so it's folding into sub-project (1)
  rather than standing alone.

  Next: what counts as "a change." This determines both which mutations the tasks core needs and the event taxonomy
  notifications will consume, so it's the last big one before I can write a design.

  - [1mField changes only[0m — status/completion, assignment, due date, title/description edits. Every one of these is a
    mutation the tasks app needs anyway; the event is just a row written in the same transaction. No new subsystem.
  - [1mField changes + comments[0m — much richer notification stream, and honestly the thing most people mean by "activity on
    a task I care about." But comments are their own subsystem: a new table, a rendering surface, editing/deleting,
    probably mentions. That's a third spec, not a line item.
  - [1mEverything including creation and deletion[0m — adds "new task in your view" and tombstone semantics. Creation events
    need a different fan-out, since nobody is subscribed to a task that doesn't exist yet; it becomes a project- or
    team-level subscription, which we don't have.

  I recommend field changes only for now. Each one maps to a mutation that already has to exist, so the event log costs
  almost nothing extra, and it's enough to prove the whole pipeline end to end: mutation → event → fan-out to
  subscribers → unread badge → mark read. Comments are the obvious next sub-project and I'd rather design them on top of
  a notifications system that already works than invent both at once. Creation/deletion I'd cut until there's a project
  or team concept to hang them on.
